jsm_apply.description:
Call one write operation from the Jira Service Management Operations API — acknowledge, close, annotate, assign, create, update or delete.

One operation per call, deliberately. The operation you are about to run appears in the arguments, so the approval your client shows names it. Do not try to batch writes through repeated calls without saying what each one will do first.

Args:
  - operation (string): the write operation to run, from the list below.
  - args (object): its arguments, snake_case, exactly as jsm_read_api reports them. Strict — an undeclared name is rejected rather than ignored.

Returns:
  {
    "operation": string,
    "result": ...,        // the API's response: a receipt for an alert action,
                          // the written object for a configuration write,
                          // { ok: true } for a 204
    "requests": number
  }

IMPORTANT — alert writes are asynchronous. Acknowledge, close, assign, snooze, escalate, annotate, tag and delete answer a receipt, { result, requestId }, confirming the request was accepted and NOT that the alert changed. Confirm with jsm_call operation="getRequestStatus". Configuration writes — schedules, rotations, teams, routing — are synchronous and answer with the object.

Read before you write. 'tinyId' is not an id: the short identifier in the JSM UI will not work here, so resolve it with listAlerts first. For a reorder or an overwrite, read the current state and tell the user what will change before calling this.

Destructive operations are DISABLED on this install. Every update and delete is refused, and the refusal names the classic tool that can do it. This is an operator setting (JSM_CODE_DESTRUCTIVE) — you cannot turn it on from a conversation, and you should not suggest the user disable a safeguard mid-task without saying plainly what it protects.

Operations available here (* marks destructive):
acknowledgeAlert, activateContact, addAlertExtraProperties*, addAlertNote, addAlertResponder, addAlertTags, assignAlert, cancelMaintenance, changeRoutingRuleOrder*, closeAlert*, createAlert, createContact, createEscalation, createMaintenance, createNotificationRule, createNotificationStep, createOverride, createRotation, createRoutingRule, createSchedule, createTeamRole, deactivateContact*, deleteAlert*, deleteAlertNote*, deleteContact*, deleteEscalation*, deleteMaintenance*, deleteNotificationRule*, deleteNotificationStep*, deleteOverride*, deleteRotation*, deleteRoutingRule*, deleteSchedule*, deleteTeamRole*, escalateAlert, executeAlertAction*, removeAlertExtraProperties*, removeAlertTags*, snoozeAlert, unacknowledgeAlert, updateAlertField*, updateAlertNote*, updateContact*, updateEscalation*, updateMaintenance*, updateNotificationRule*, updateNotificationStep*, updateOverride*, updateRotation*, updateRoutingRule*, updateSchedule*, updateTeamRole*

Examples:
  - "Ack the Redis alert, I'm on it" -> resolve the id with jsm_call listAlerts, then operation="acknowledgeAlert", args={"alert_id":"<full id>"}
  - "Add a note to that alert" -> operation="addAlertNote", args={"alert_id":"<id>","note":"Investigating"}
  - "Put Sam on call for Saturday" -> operation="createOverride", args={...}

jsm_run.description:
Run a JavaScript snippet that reads from the Jira Service Management Operations API, and get back only what it logs.

Use this instead of jsm_call whenever the answer is smaller than the data it comes from: a count, an extreme, a join across two families, a filter over more records than you want to read. The script runs next to the API, so a sweep over two thousand alerts costs you the one line you log about it rather than two thousand records of context.

Args:
  - script (string): JavaScript. Top-level `await` works. Up to 65,536 characters.

How to write one:
  - `await jsm.<operation>(args)` calls one operation and returns the API's data. Arguments are the same snake_case ones jsm_read_api documents. A collection comes back as `{ items, total?, nextOffset?, nextCursor? }`.
  - `await jsm.listAll<Thing>(args, { max })` reads every page and returns `{ items, pages, complete, stoppedBecause? }`. **Check `complete`** — false means a budget stopped the sweep early and `items` is partial.
  - `console.log(...)` is the only way anything reaches you. A script that computes the right answer and logs nothing returns nothing. Log a summary: a single line is cut at 20,000 characters.
  - The script's whole world is `jsm`, `console` and the JavaScript language itself. There is no `require`, `import`, `fetch`, `process`, `setTimeout` or `eval` — not blocked, absent — so do not reach for them.
  - A failed operation throws, and the error carries `.status`, `.operation` and `.retryable` alongside its message, so `catch (e) { if (e.status === 404) ... }` works.

Returns: the script's log lines, in order, trimmed to 25,000 characters. On failure, the lines logged before it failed plus one error saying which kind it was: a syntax error (fix the script), a thrown error (fix the logic), a budget (narrow the work), or an operation error (read the API's own advice in the message).

The sandbox: the script runs in its own empty JavaScript realm, inside a separate process that has no filesystem, no network, no ability to start anything, and an empty environment — it cannot reach the Atlassian credential, and it could not send anything anywhere if it had it. Every API call it makes is a message to this server, which makes the request. There is no state between runs: each script gets a fresh process.

**It can only read.** Every operation below issues a GET. To change something, use jsm_apply, which takes one operation per call so your client can approve it by name.

Budgets, shared across the whole script: 60 HTTP requests, 5,000 records, 50 pages per auto-paged read, 4 concurrent requests, 10000ms. A budget reached mid-script is reported as a budget error, and anything logged before it still comes back.

Examples:

  "How many schedules are disabled?"
    const all = await jsm.listAllSchedules({});
    const off = all.items.filter(s => s.enabled === false);
    console.log(`${off.length} of ${all.items.length} schedules are disabled`);
    console.log(off.map(s => s.name).join(", "));

  "Which schedule has nobody on call right now?"
    const { items: schedules } = await jsm.listSchedules({ limit: 100 });
    const gaps = [];
    for (const s of schedules) {
      const answer = await jsm.getOnCall({ schedule_id: s.id });
      if (!answer.participants?.length) gaps.push(s.name ?? s.id);
    }
    console.log(gaps.length ? `Nobody on call for: ${gaps.join(", ")}` : "Every schedule is covered");

  "Which open P1 has the highest dedupe count?"
    const all = await jsm.listAllAlerts({ query: "status:open AND priority:P1" }, { max: 2000 });
    if (!all.complete) console.log(`Partial: ${all.stoppedBecause}`);
    const worst = all.items.reduce((a, b) => ((b.count ?? 0) > (a.count ?? 0) ? b : a));
    console.log(`${worst.tinyId} — ${worst.message} (count ${worst.count})`);

Operations you can call (29), as `await jsm.<name>(args)`:
getAlert, getContact, getEscalation, getMaintenance, getNextOnCall, getNotificationRule, getNotificationStep, getOnCall, getOverride, getRequestStatus, getRotation, getRoutingRule, getSchedule, getScheduleTimeline, getTeamRole, listAlertLogs, listAlertNotes, listAlerts, listContacts, listEscalations, listMaintenances, listNotificationRules, listNotificationSteps, listOverrides, listRotations, listRoutingRules, listSchedules, listTeamRoles, listTeams

Auto-paged forms (12):
listAllAlertLogs, listAllAlertNotes, listAllAlerts, listAllContacts, listAllEscalations, listAllMaintenances, listAllNotificationRules, listAllNotificationSteps, listAllOverrides, listAllRotations, listAllRoutingRules, listAllSchedules

Call jsm_read_api for an operation's exact arguments before guessing them, and jsm_search_api if you cannot see the operation you want.
